# ============================================================================
# .gitignore — Prevent Secrets, Build Artifacts, and Environment-Specific
# Files from Being Committed to Version Control
#
# This file is part of the security hardening for march_repo_hello_world.
# It ensures that sensitive credentials (TLS private keys, environment
# secrets), large reproducible artifacts (node_modules), and developer-
# local files (editor configs, OS metadata) are never tracked by Git.
# ============================================================================

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 1: Node.js / npm Artifacts
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# npm dependencies directory — can contain thousands of files and is fully
# reproducible from package.json + package-lock.json via `npm ci`.
node_modules/

# npm debug and error logs generated during install or script execution.
npm-debug.log*

# Yarn package manager logs (included for future compatibility if the
# project ever migrates from npm to yarn).
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 2: Environment Files (Secrets Protection)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The active .env file contains real configuration values including secrets
# such as TLS key paths, CORS origins, and rate limit settings.
# CRITICAL: This file MUST never be committed to version control.
.env

# Local environment overrides used by developers for machine-specific config.
.env.local

# Environment-specific local overrides (e.g., .env.development.local,
# .env.production.local). These may contain credentials or tokens.
.env.*.local

# NOTE: .env.example is intentionally NOT listed here — it is the template
# file that documents all required environment variables WITHOUT actual
# secret values, and it MUST be tracked in version control.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 3: TLS Certificate Private Keys (Critical Security)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PEM-encoded certificate files generated for HTTPS/TLS support.
# These may contain the server certificate or certificate chain.
certs/*.pem

# Private key files used for TLS encryption. Private keys are the most
# sensitive cryptographic material in the project — committing them to
# version control would compromise all encrypted communications.
# RATIONALE: Private keys MUST never be committed to version control.
certs/*.key

# NOTE: The certs/generate-cert.sh script is intentionally NOT listed here —
# it is source code (not a secret) and MUST be tracked in version control
# so that developers can regenerate self-signed certificates locally.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 4: OS-Generated Files
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# macOS Finder directory metadata files.
.DS_Store

# Windows Explorer thumbnail cache database files.
Thumbs.db

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 5: Editor and IDE Configuration Files
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Visual Studio Code workspace settings and configuration.
.vscode/

# JetBrains IDE (WebStorm, IntelliJ IDEA) project settings.
.idea/

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 6: Coverage and Test Output
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test coverage reports generated by test runners (Jest, Istanbul/nyc, etc.).
# These are build artifacts that should be regenerated locally, not tracked.
coverage/
